WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.045 --> 00:00:02.885
(futuristic electronic music)

00:00:02.885 --> 00:00:05.069
- I've always had that inner fire in me

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:08.319
like I gotta prove 'em wrong, you know?

00:00:11.654 --> 00:00:14.511
That comes from, I think, my
hip hop spirit a little bit.

00:00:14.511 --> 00:00:17.046
I'm not gonna wait for that
mic to be passed to me.

00:00:17.046 --> 00:00:18.950
I have to like grab it.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:22.367
(funky electronic music)

00:00:23.615 --> 00:00:26.126
My name is Jonathan Park,
also known as Dumbfoundead,

00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:30.274
a rapper based out of
Los Angeles, California.

00:00:30.274 --> 00:00:32.612
I grew up in front of the television, you know.

00:00:32.612 --> 00:00:34.450
My whole personality is based off of like

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.618
sitcoms from the 90s, you know?

00:00:37.618 --> 00:00:40.866
I started having interests
in entertainment.

00:00:40.866 --> 00:00:42.275
It started getting a little discouraging

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:44.826
'cause I wouldn't see anybody
on television or films

00:00:44.826 --> 00:00:46.010
that looked like me.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:49.178
And that's when I started going
a little bit more aggressive

00:00:49.178 --> 00:00:50.928
with everything I do.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.124
♫ Yeah, Daddy was a G

00:00:54.124 --> 00:00:55.795
♫ Granddaddy was a G, too

00:00:55.795 --> 00:00:56.954
♫ They ain't never bang ♫

00:00:56.954 --> 00:00:59.714
When I started hopping on
stages, when I got a microphone,

00:00:59.714 --> 00:01:01.978
I wanted to be loud, and
I wanted to be heard,

00:01:01.978 --> 00:01:05.995
and I wanted to be outlandish
and say crazy stuff, you know?

00:01:05.995 --> 00:01:08.468
And that's kind of what
built this confidence in me

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:12.042
to pursue rap and rap the
things that I wanna talk about.

00:01:12.042 --> 00:01:15.810
♫ You took me as safe ♫

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.730
The song and music video, Safe,
is pretty much my response

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.014
to the whitewashing of Asians in Hollywood

00:01:22.014 --> 00:01:25.150
or any minority in the
entertainment industry,

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.958
and I wanted to kind of come
at it aggressively on the song

00:01:27.958 --> 00:01:29.839
that nah, we aren’t, you know, the model minority.

00:01:29.839 --> 00:01:32.671
We have things to say, and
you gonna hear a rebuttal

00:01:32.671 --> 00:01:33.795
if you attack us.

00:01:33.795 --> 00:01:37.638
("Safe" by Dumbfoundead)

00:01:37.638 --> 00:01:39.742
(soft instrumental music)

00:01:39.742 --> 00:01:42.100
Growing up with a very
traditional Korean-American family

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.531
in the household, being
involved in music and arts,

00:01:44.531 --> 00:01:46.212
that was a rare thing at the time

00:01:46.212 --> 00:01:48.545
for Asian parents to accept.

00:01:50.164 --> 00:01:53.956
When they turned on the television
and saw no Asians on TV,

00:01:53.956 --> 00:01:55.740
they were like, there's no money in this

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.490
You know what I mean?

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:01.716
But growing up in Koreatown
gave me tons of confidence,

00:02:01.716 --> 00:02:04.571
confidence of who I am as a person,

00:02:04.571 --> 00:02:06.620
being a strong Korean-American

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.476
without feeling like an outsider.

00:02:08.476 --> 00:02:11.251
And that's a responsibility on its own

00:02:11.251 --> 00:02:12.900
to take that influence around me

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.611
and translate it into my art

00:02:14.611 --> 00:02:18.778
and share these stories of my
people all across, you know?

